1921:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp **** Important properties of s_out:
1922:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp **** * it contains alphanumeric characters, dots, underscores, minus signs
1923:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp **** * these symbols are grouped into words, which are separated by spaces
1924:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   and Windows-style (CR+LF) newlines
1925:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp **** * although  serializer  uses  spaces and CR+LF as separators, you can 
1926:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   replace any separator character by arbitrary combination of spaces,
1927:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   tabs, Windows or Unix newlines. It allows flexible reformatting  of
1928:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   the  string in case you want to include it into a text or XML file. 
1929:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   But you should not insert separators into the middle of the "words"
1930:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   nor should you change the case of letters.
1931:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp **** * s_out can be freely moved between 32-bit and 64-bit systems, little
1932:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   and big endian machines, and so on. You can serialize structure  on
1933:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   32-bit machine and unserialize it on 64-bit one (or vice versa), or
1934:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   serialize  it  on  SPARC  and  unserialize  on  x86.  You  can also 
1935:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   serialize it in C++ version of ALGLIB and unserialize it in C# one, 
1936:libs/alglib/src/alglibmisc.cpp ****   and vice versa.
